My project involves training a 4x4 matrix to generate a clean and accurate NDVI image from a cost effective MIRA 220 CAMERA that has a significant spectral overlap based on images captured from a MAP IR Survey 3 Cameras+ reflecteance calibration kit, post processed with chloros. Use MAP IR NDVI as ground truth.

From MIRA 220: 03_color_corrected_Bilinear_Debayering.png that is a debayered and corrected using a color card. This is a png file. The code for how this is generated from a raw file is in mira_pipeline.py.The python code takes a raw files named Vijay_0315_leaves_pic.raw,dark.raw (captured with lid closed to remove noise/ leakage from pixels) and white.raw that is a
raw file captured under the same light conditions as vijay_0315_leaves_pic.raw. his should be used for calibrating the intensity and removing noise. The pixel pattern on the camera is [B G R G;G I G I;R G B G;G I G I]. We have to flip pixel by pixel horizontally not the entire image. The data sheet for the Camera is available at Mira220_DS000642_9-00.pdf. As seen from page of the manual there is a significant spectral overlap between the R G B- IR that can give a wrong NDVI plot for the vegetation and lead us to wrong conclusion about the plant's health. We have used a color card to calibrate the MIRA 220 image and obtained a 4x4 matrix that is used to reduce spectral over lap.The 4x4 matrix is included in the mira_pipeline.py. So the original raw file is corrected using the dark and white calibration and subsequently with the matrix. This gives a raw file from which we can calculate the NDVI data. This NDVI is not accurate. So we use a more expensive camera , MAP IR camera in this case /www.mapir.camera/pages/processing-survey3-camera-images and a reflectance calibration set. We take pictures of the same vegetation oriented in the same way with the MIRA 220 camera and MAP IR Camera. The reflectance calibration is also included in the image. Chloros was downloaded from map IR website and was used to process the images and reflectance calibration to get a .tiff file Vijay_0315_leaves_tif.tif. The Chloros also calculates NDVI. The data is in RGN Format.

 Now we have 03_color_corrected_Bilinear_Debayering.png, Raw file that is calibrated and color corrected with 4X4 matrix from the mira_pipeline.py program (from MIRA 220) and Vijay_0315_leaves_tif.tif (from MAPIR). We need to use the NDVI from the MAPIR as the ground truth, align the images I mentioned using the square found in the reflectance Calibration (arUco markers), from both the cameras. Pick just the vegetation part NDVI>0.4 from MAP IR + Chloros and compare it with the corresponding NDVI generated from MIRA 220 (from mira_pipeline.py). These two images may not have the same scales or pixels. Hence have to be aligned using the arUco markers

Ultimately we will capture a lot of images from the MAP IR and MIRA 220 along with the reflectance calibration. We have to align these images using the squares/ arUco markers and then used the MAP IR NDVI as ground truth and adapt the 4x4 matrix  
with a Machine Learning algorithm to make the images from MIRA more accurate. The code in arucodetect.py worked to detect the marker from Vijay_0315_leaves_tif.tif - so that logic can be used to detect the aruco markers. this will be needed to align the images taken from Mira220 and the Map IR camera.
